    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "de9d9701_4b192242",
        "filename": "zephyr/dts/bindings/power/intel,ap-pwrseq.yaml",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 157,
      "author": {
        "id": 1281040
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-02-24T20:52:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So I\u0027m not too familiar with the virtual wire stuff, but yes, it would certainly be possible to wrap a gpio controller driver around it and present these bits as a normalised GPIO API.\nThis may be a good upstream project. I\u0027ll look into it.\n\nFor this particular instance, the goal here is to abstract all the power signals behind an access layer so that the power sequence code does not know the details of how the signal value is obtained - this way, we can use GPIOs, virtual wire, and board level special code without the power sequence code having to deal with each individually.\nThe board level code is a good example of this benefit - for Nissa the signal retrieved is the PP33000 power good signal, but this is actually read from an ADC, so we can push that complexity into the board level function.",
      "parentUuid": "d5b48347_b8048b19",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 157,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 157,
        "endChar": 21
      },
